## Status legend

- **filled** —
  - *Content sections (project / technical / RAM):* required content present, no `[FILL_IN]` markers remain.
  - *Tool-integration sections:* every canonical tier from the install matrix (see `docs/integration-architecture.md` § *Recommended install ladders*) is present on disk, OR each pre-selected tier was explicitly declined by the user during the matrix step (note declines in `Notes`).
- **partial** — content present but incomplete.
  - *Tool-integration sections:* this is the **default** if any canonical tier is silently absent — not installed AND not explicitly declined. Enumerate what's missing in `Notes`. Burden of proof is on `filled`.
- **postponed** — user explicitly said "come back to this".
- **skipped** — user explicitly said "I don't want this".
- **not_started** — file exists as skeleton but no user content yet.

## `memory-bank/.install/` — install-time scaffolding

The `memory-bank/.install/` directory (present after `dmb init`) holds reference templates the AI copies from during Section 7 of the install interview. It's **not** part of your project's knowledge layer and you don't normally touch it.

After Section 7 finishes (or whenever you're satisfied with your integration setup), three reasonable choices:

- **Keep it** — useful if you'll later add another tool's integration via *"continue DMB install"*. Lowest friction for future use.
- **Add `.install/` to `.gitignore`** (recommended) — keeps the templates locally available without bloating git history. Future re-runs of `dmb init` will refresh them.
- **Delete it** — works if you're done customising and won't add tool integrations later. `dmb init --force` would re-create it if needed.

## How the AI fills the status for integration sections

After running the Section 7 matrix install for a tool (see `INSTALL.md` § *Matrix install*):

1. User said *"looks good"* and all ✓ items were installed → **filled**. `Notes` mentions any optional `—` items the user added.
2. User explicitly declined some ✓ item(s) → **filled** still. `Notes` enumerates the declines (e.g. *"Skills declined; user prefers path-scoped rules only."*).
3. Any ✓ item is silently absent (not installed AND not explicitly declined) → **partial**. `Notes` lists what's missing.

This rule exists because silent skipping of canonical tiers leaves users with the least-reliable mechanism by default while telling them they're "fully installed." The matrix flow plus this status rule together prevent that.

